"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', a next-generation open-weight multimodal model featuring 1 trillion parameters and 52 billion active parameters. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a native multimodal model that delivers state-of-the-art open model performance across enterprise environments, including coding, agent workflows, cybersecurity, and finance. Trained from scratch using 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its proprietary European data centers, it incorporates training data spanning over 160 languages. Currently available as a preview API via Mistral Studio, the model weights are scheduled for public release at the end of this month following red-teaming and related testing. Introducing Timestral Mistral has unveiled 'Timestral', a family of time-series foundation models offering outstanding efficiency and zero-shot performance. With a maximum size of 209M parameters, it achieved first place on three major benchmarks—GIFT-Eval, TIME, and fev-bench—using only two-thirds of the parameters of previous top-performing models. Available in four sizes (5M, 28M, 123M, and 209M), it runs efficiently even on CPUs and delivers an optimal balance between accuracy and computational cost. Combining synthetic data pretraining, covariate-aware attention, and long-context training, it supports multivariate probabilistic forecasting with both past and future covariates. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral establishes a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest industrial economy—and officially expands into Physics AI and industrial AI sectors. Mistral opened its Munich hub to bolster competitiveness by combining its capabilities with Germany's manufacturing and industrial expertise across automotive, energy, and aerospace sectors. Building on physics and engineering AI specialists who joined following the acquisition of Emmi AI in May 2026, Mistral is co-developing crash simulations with BMW and industrial AI applications with Siemens Energy. To secure European AI sovereignty, Mistral aims to build 1 gigawatt (GW) of European computing infrastructure by 2030 and provide open-weight proprietary model environments running on customer infrastructure. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla collaborate to integrate Mistral models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window'. Mozilla's AI browsing assistant 'Firefox Smart Window (beta)' is powered by Mistral models. It will be rolled out first to users in France and North America, with plans to expand to the UK and Germany later this year. It offers multilingual support optimized for local languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, along with privacy features based on the principle of not storing conversations on Mozilla's servers and zero data retention. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral AI and Cloudera collaborate to deliver sovereign AI intelligence specialized for enterprise data and support model deployment across on-premises and hybrid environments. Mistral's models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, enabling inference execution across public and private clouds, on-premises, and fully air-gapped (network-isolated) environments. By building custom models utilizing large-scale proprietary data within controlled environments, enterprises can maintain complete ownership of their data and models. Supports innovation for enterprises in regulated industries by meeting the demand for sovereign AI that places data, intelligence, compute, and operations under the customer's full control. Dial Up the Cloud: NVIDIA DLSS 4.5 Controls Arrive on GeForce NOW NVIDIA has introduced detailed DLSS 4.5 controls and new titles to its GeForce NOW cloud gaming service. NVIDIA DLSS 4.5 control options have been added to GeForce NOW, enabling direct cloud-based fine-tuning of Super Resolution, Dynamic Multi Frame Generation, Ray Reconstruction, and more Ultimate membership members utilizing the GeForce RTX 5080-based cloud environment can take advantage of top-tier options, including up to 4X Dynamic Multi Frame Generation Ten new games have been added to the streaming lineup, including the official release version of Enshrouded 1.0 and Warhammer 40,000: Boltgun 2 NVIDIA Expands Advanced Technology Access for Europe’s Scientific Community Through Open Learning Resources, Collaborations and Compute NVIDIA joins the European Commission's 'AI in Science Enablers' initiative, expanding support for open-source models, curriculum, and compute resources for European scientific researchers and startups. NVIDIA has joined the 'AI in Science Enablers' initiative launched by the European Commission, pledging support with tools, models, cloud resources, and technical training. Providing over 250 open models and datasets alongside more than 1,400 open-source software packages to support science-specialized AI ecosystems such as Nemotron, BioNeMo, and Earth-2. Opening free open-source training courses through the NVIDIA Deep Learning Institute (DLI), and expanding local compute access via national AI factories and the Inception program in countries including France, Spain, and Sweden. The Nordics and NVIDIA Show What’s Possible When a Region Commits to Advanced Computing Powered by abundant renewable energy and NVIDIA's advanced computing infrastructure, the Nordic countries are rapidly emerging as a major AI ecosystem and infrastructure hub in Europe. The Nordic region plans to expand AI cloud infrastructure to a capacity of 1 GW by the end of 2027, led by cloud companies such as Bitdeer and CoreWeave. A consortium of leading Swedish companies has built 'Sferical One,' a shared supercomputer based on NVIDIA DGX SuperPOD, and is leveraging Nemotron open models. Major local enterprises such as Spotify, ABB Robotics, and Elisa are adopting NVIDIA infrastructure and software to implement various AI solutions, including recommendation services, robotics simulations, and enterprise agents. Cohere to acquire AISLE to advance cyber-resilient sovereign AI Cohere has agreed to acquire AISLE, an autonomous vulnerability detection and remediation startup, to build a cyber-resilient sovereign AI platform and strengthen its security capabilities. Cohere announced its intent to acquire AISLE to complete a sovereign AI platform combining advanced AI models with security infrastructure, and to expand its presence in Europe. In its first year out of stealth, AISLE discovered more than 500 previously unknown CVE vulnerabilities across over 100 codebases, including zero-day vulnerabilities in OpenSSL and curl. AISLE's three co-founders will join key leadership roles as Head of Cohere Cyber, CISO, and VP of AI Security, respectively, with plans to develop Prague into an AI cybersecurity hub. AI agents are coming to Rivian’s cars Rivian announced plans to integrate agents from external frontier AI companies such as Anthropic into its electric vehicles, powered by its own in-house agent framework. Rivian CEO RJ Scaringe stated plans to support running AI agents from external companies like Anthropic in their electric vehicles going forward. Using its proprietary agent framework, requests will be routed to either vehicle on-device (edge) models or cloud models based on task complexity. Safety-critical core vehicle control functions such as braking and steering will not be delegated to agents; implementation will focus primarily on convenience features, scheduling, and contextual integration. Google’s Universal Cart shopping tool launches in the US Google has launched 'Universal Cart' in the US, a shopping tool across Search and the Gemini app that aggregates products from multiple retailers, manages them, and supports checkout. Users can aggregate and manage items discovered across Google Search and the Gemini app from multiple online stores into a single cart and maintain their purchase progress. It provides personalized shopping assistance features such as price-drop alerts for saved items, restock notifications, payment recommendations based on credit card benefits, and promotional codes. Checkout is integrated based on the Universal Commerce Protocol (UCP), an open standard that facilitates seamless transactions between AI agents and retailers. TechCrunch Disrupt 2026: Last day Guide to major sessions and startup events on the final day of TechCrunch Disrupt 2026, a global tech conference held in San Francisco. The final third-day schedule of TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 takes place at Moscone West in San Francisco, covering AI innovation, startup growth, fundraising, and more. Features exhibitions from over 320 startups, the 1-minute pitch stage Startup Battlefield 200, and a networking environment powered by Klik smart badges. Hosts sessions on AI startups and early-stage founding strategies featuring investors and key personnel from leading companies such as NVIDIA, Amazon, and Bluesky Social. Figma announces new features to define how agents work on its platform At the Config event in India, Figma announced new features, including Figma Specs—a Markdown-based editor that helps AI agents follow design rules—and Code Layers for interactive prototypes. Launched the Figma Specs Markdown editor to centralize design system context and help teams and AI agents consistently follow brand rules Moved Code Layers to public beta, enabling interactive prototyping by adding coding elements directly to canvas designs Supported over 20 new tools including Open Code, Dia, Grok Bot, and Sarvam via MCP (Model Context Protocol), and integrated previously acquired headless CMS Payload Gudea leverages AI to help organizations prevent social media crises before they erupt Claiming to be a storm tracker for the internet, Gudea leverages AI to detect the spread of online rumors and issues, helping organizations proactively prevent social media crises. Gudea describes itself as a 'storm tracker for the internet' that tracks how stories and rumors spread online Based on AI technology, it detects emerging online issues early to help organizations respond before they escalate into serious crises How Windows is being optimized for Nvidia’s RTX Spark laptops Microsoft is building a Windows environment optimized for running on-device AI models and agents tailored for Nvidia's Arm-based RTX Spark laptops. Nvidia's new RTX Spark platform combines an Arm CPU, Blackwell GPU, and unified memory to support running powerful local AI models on laptops. Microsoft has introduced a new out-of-box setup experience that allows users to install and run AI agents through the Windows Store in just two clicks. At launch, three agents—Hermes, Perplexity, and OpenClaw—are officially supported, with additional agents like Meta's Muse slated for future support. Nobody is safe from celebrity AI deepfakes AI deepfake images and videos misappropriating celebrity likenesses are spreading on a massive scale by exploiting loopholes in social media verification badge systems, causing severe harm. Shortly after actress Mia Goth's divorce, a fan account bearing a paid Instagram blue verification checkmark posted fabricated AI images that garnered hundreds of thousands of 'likes' and spread across the entertainment industry and search engines. As Meta operates a paid verification badge system, non-public figures' accounts have been mistaken for official accounts, leading to manipulated AI content gaining credibility and circulating widely. As seen in cases involving Scarlett Johansson, Drake, and others, pushback and legal action from celebrities against impersonation and digital replication using generative AI continue to mount. Google launches its 'universal' online shopping cart ahead of the holidays Ahead of the holiday shopping season, Google has launched an AI-powered 'universal digital cart' feature that lets users collect and manage items from multiple shopping sites in one place. The 'Universal Cart' feature, which enables users to save items from multiple retailers into a single shopping cart across Google Search and the Gemini app, has launched in the US. It provides price-drop and back-in-stock alerts for cart items, displays promotional codes, and supports payments with insights on optimal credit card benefits. As big tech companies such as OpenAI, Meta, and Shopify accelerate competition in AI-powered shopping agents, Google is also mounting a serious push into the market. openKylin Outlines Its Vision for Reimagining Linux in the AI Era at Open Source Summit Europe 2026 openKylin unveiled its vision for a next-generation Linux operating system powered by an AI runtime and system agent environment at Open Source Summit Europe 2026. openKylin proposed a paradigm shift from traditional 'resource management'-centric operating systems to 'intent management'-centric systems that understand and execute user intent. The team introduced a system-level agent execution environment architecture supporting the unified AI runtime KART, local inference engine kytensor, KylinBot, and Kylin-CUA. openKylin presented openKylin 3.0's AI assistant and global intelligent search features, along with 2–5x FP32 inference acceleration achieved through RISC-V hardware and RVV optimizations. TypeSafe AI Releases Jev 1.14 With Images, Box Type and 262k Context TypeSafe AI has unveiled Jev 1.14, a decision model that adds image input support, the Box question type, and context expansion up to 262k. TypeSafe AI has launched version 1.14 of Jev, a System One model that returns typed decision values instead of generating text, under jev-preview. Three experimental features have been added: image input, the Box type returning bounding box coordinates (0–1 ratios), and long context expandable up to 262k tokens. All new features are not yet suitable for production environments and are offered exclusively via a hosted API (POST /v1/systemone). Meet Grok Voice 3.0: SpaceXAI Voice Agent Model Ranks #1 on Artificial Analysis SpaceXAI has launched Grok Voice 3.0, a voice agent model with real-time voice interaction and reasoning capabilities, ranking #1 on the Artificial Analysis benchmark. Grok Voice 3.0 is a speech-to-speech model supporting listening, reasoning, tool calling, and speaking, which achieved first place with a score of 86.9 on the Artificial Analysis Index. High-level reasoning is enabled by default, supporting in-session tool execution such as web search, X search, and remote MCP server integration. It achieved a low latency of 0.81 seconds to first audio output, with an API usage fee of $0.08 per minute, the same as the previous version. Building AI Applications with OGX, the Open-Source Agentic API Server Introduces the features of OGX, an open-source agentic API server rebranded from Meta's Llama Stack, and demonstrates how to build AI applications using it. OGX is an open-source agentic API server rebranded from Meta's Llama Stack, offering model neutrality and supporting a production-ready multi-SDK environment. It provides an OpenAI-compatible API, allowing developers to build with a unified interface while seamlessly swapping backends across local (Ollama), production (vLLM), and cloud environments. It supports server-side agent orchestration, tool calling, MCP server integration, and file retrieval for RAG through a single API call. 7 Open-Source AI Tools That Can Replace Paid Subscriptions Introduces seven open-source AI tools that can replace paid AI subscription services to enhance privacy, regain control, and reduce costs. Paid services such as OpenAI API, Midjourney, Claude Code, Notion, Wispr Flow, Grammarly, and NotebookLM can be replaced with local or self-hosted open-source tools. llama.cpp efficiently runs quantized models on diverse hardware including CPUs and GPUs, providing an OpenAI-compatible API and web UI to reduce paid LLM subscription and token costs. ComfyUI is a node-based open-source tool for image and video generation that replaces Midjourney or Adobe Firefly, offering detailed control capabilities. Artificiality's First Exam: An LLM-Referenced C-test for Supraintelligence This study proposes a new C-test benchmark combining Large Language Models (LLMs) and Levin's Kt complexity to evaluate supraintelligence that surpasses the capabilities of humans and existing AI. Existing benchmarks have limitations in that their difficulty levels are calibrated against humans or current AI, leading to rapid saturation or circular referencing issues. By combining Levin's Kt—a computable variant of Kolmogorov complexity—with LLMs as representation machines, the authors established a pipeline to generate and verify high-difficulty evaluation questions measured on a ratio scale in bits. Evaluating certified questions across 12 LLMs confirmed performance degradation curves as difficulty increased, demonstrating the feasibility of evaluating questions in the supraintelligence domain beyond current frontier intelligence. The Silent Failures in Agentic Data Science This study points out and systematically analyzes methodological flaws, such as data leakage and validation errors, hidden beneath the surface of simple predictive accuracy evaluations in existing data science agents. Conventional evaluations of downstream predictive performance and code correctness alone fail to detect methodological failures such as data leakage and improper validation. An analysis of 220 data science tasks revealed that regardless of the complexity of the agent framework or model performance, at least one failure mode was found across the vast majority of agent solutions. A positive correlation was observed between the occurrence of data leakage flaws and predictive performance, proving that a principled analysis of defects beyond mere accuracy is essential. Compared to expert solutions, such as those from Kaggle competition winners, data science agents were found to generally lack problem-solving creativity and domain expertise. ClawLoop: Self-Improving Agents via Harness-Native Training Loop This study proposes ClawLoop, a harness-native self-improving agent framework that generates tasks, trains, diagnoses, and enhances its performance autonomously without external teacher models or human intervention. Introduces a self-feedback iterative structure to overcome the external teacher dependency and one-shot limitations of existing harness-based agent training. ClawLoop operates through a 3-stage continuous loop: Task Synthesis, Model Training (SFT/RL), and Diagnosis and Feedback. Tasks refined through the loop model transferred effectively to smaller models, proving the versatility and high quality of the synthesized data. Correction Training for Diffusion Models This study proposes Correction Training, a framework for diffusion models that bridges the exposure bias gap between training and inference to improve generation quality and prevent error accumulation. Introduces a corrector step to refine intermediate predictions, mitigating exposure bias and error accumulation during the iterative sampling process of diffusion models. Significantly improves FID scores for unconditional and unguided generation across DiT, SiT, and JiT architectures, while alleviating oversaturation and enhancing diversity when combined with classifier-free guidance (CFG). Demonstrates that distilling the refined predictions back into the base diffusion model maintains the improved quality without incurring additional inference-time overhead. Long-Horizon-Terminal-Bench 2.0: Automating Diverse Task Construction and Measuring Sustained Agent Progress LHTB 2.0, a benchmark designed to evaluate AI agents' ability to sustain continuous task execution in long-horizon terminal environments, has been released. LHTB 2.0 was constructed by expanding the existing 46 tasks with 31 new tasks, resulting in a total of 77 tasks across 13 domains and 8 task types. The evaluation duration was extended from 90 minutes to 3 hours, recording verification checkpoints every 30 minutes to measure intermediate progress via dense progress ladders. Evaluating 15 frontier models showed that GPT-6-astra scored the highest, demonstrating that outcome-only evaluations substantially overlook intermediate progress compared to fine-grained evaluation scores (0.653 vs. a simple binary evaluation of 0.221). Lotus: Learning to Understand Time-series by Learning to Forecast Proposes the principle of 'forecast grounding', which pre-trains time-series language models to maintain future-forecasting capabilities, alongside the LOTUS model that implements it. To overcome the limitation of existing time-series language models failing to preserve the evolutionary dynamics of time-series signals, introduced the forecast grounding principle that maintains future predictability prior to downstream task adaptation. LOTUS partitions time-series channels into patches, maps them into the LLM embedding space, predicts the next patch, and employs a time-spectral patch representation method that simultaneously captures temporal dynamics and periodic patterns. Achieved the highest average performance across 3 language model backbones and 6 benchmark datasets with minimal trainable parameters, outperforming models 25 to 50 times larger on TSRBench. Inverse Reasoning Models for Efficient Post-Training and Verification from Answer-Only Data A study on an inverse reasoning model-based self-distillation technique that treats reasoning paths as latent variables to perform efficient post-training and verification using answer-only data. A framework was proposed that enables offline self-distillation even with answer-only data by utilizing a partner inverse reasoning LLM that generates backward reasoning paths conditioned on the answers. In experiments using the Qwen3-0.6B model, a single round of offline distillation nearly doubled the pass@1 accuracy on challenging problems, even after GRPO training had saturated. The trained inverse reasoning model functions as an accurate verifier at test time, significantly improving selection accuracy in best-of-n sampling compared to majority voting. PMO-Dock: Benchmarking Docking, Specificity, and Generalization in Molecular Optimization This paper proposes PMO-Dock, a novel molecular optimization benchmark designed to reflect structure-based drug discovery settings and fairly evaluate docking, target specificity, and generalization performance on unseen tasks. Overcoming the limitations of conventional PMO benchmarks based solely on simple physical properties, PMO-Dock establishes 25 docking tasks that encompass hit discovery, lead optimization, and target specificity tasks accounting for off-target interaction penalties. To reflect realistic drug discovery settings, a standardized protocol was introduced with strict oracle call budget constraints and a clear separation between validation tasks for hyperparameter tuning and test tasks for final evaluation. Benchmarking four optimization paradigms—reinforcement learning (Saturn), discrete diffusion models (GenMol), genetic-guided GFlowNet, and LLM-based evolutionary search (ChemLlama)—revealed that no single model consistently outperformed others across all tasks, highlighting the need for tailored strategies per methodology due to hyperparameter transferability limits. Scaling Cheap Synthetic Pretraining Improves Indoor Radio-Map Prediction This study significantly improves indoor radio-frequency path loss (Radio-Map) prediction performance by pretraining on large-scale, low-cost synthetic data leveraging diverse indoor layout generators and a fast open-source ray tracer. To address the high cost of real-world measurement data and precision ray-tracing simulations, the researchers introduced an indoor layout generator and a fast approximate ray tracer to build a large-scale synthetic dataset of 128M samples. After pretraining and fine-tuning ResNet-101 and Swin-T encoder-decoder models, the ResNet-101 model reduced the average error by 15% across five indoor radio-map prediction tasks compared to prior state-of-the-art methods, while also improving transfer performance on small-scale real-world measurements. Using a power-law fit on 2M to 16M samples, the average normalized RMSE across the 32M to 128M range was predicted with high accuracy (a MAPE of 3.33% for ResNet-101), confirming clear data scaling laws. IR-PRM+ with Previous-Utility Prediction: Constant Regret and Best-Iterate Convergence This study achieves constant regret and best-iterate convergence guarantees in two-player zero-sum games using the IR-PRM+ algorithm combined with a previous-utility prediction technique. By introducing previous-utility prediction into IR-PRM+, which supports simultaneous and alternating updates, the theoretical convergence guarantees of the conventional extragradient method were secured. It proves a T-independent external regret upper bound, an O(T^{-1}) average duality gap, an O(T^{-1/2}) best-iterate gap, and demonstrates convergence to Nash equilibrium. By jointly analyzing the relative growth rate of non-decreasing regret vector norms, unbounded divergence of norms is prevented, and this is extended to the CFR variant IR-PCFR+ and extensive-form games. Following the Flow the Law Requires: Training Voice Agents to Transfer to Unseen Regulated Procedures in Debt Collection This research presents a framework for training voice AI agents to strictly adhere to legally regulated conversational procedures, such as debt collection, even in novel regulatory environments not encountered during prior training. Proposed 'FlowSpec,' a machine-readable specification that defines guideline generation, tool control, supervision, and testing to ensure agents comply with statutory and creditor-specific procedures in heavily regulated domains. Fine-tuned a 9-billion-parameter (9B) model using 2,506 conversational datasets generated across 297 randomized procedures, evaluating it on four unseen Thai outbound call procedures (three debt collection, one non-debt). Training results demonstrated a significant increase in procedural compliance (Documented checks) from 67–71% to 93–100%, alongside a substantial improvement in scenario pass rate from 15/43 to 40/43. Evaluating Large Language Models for Multilingual Abstractive Summarization: A Comprehensive Study of English and Bengali A comprehensive comparative study evaluating the multilingual abstractive summarization performance and factual consistency of major large language models across English and Bengali, a low-resource language. Analyzed the zero-shot performance of eight commercial and open-source LLMs on equally sized English and Bengali XL-Sum test sets by combining lexical and semantic metrics, factuality verification, and LLM-as-a-judge evaluation. In contrast to GPT-5.6 Luna, which showed strong performance across English metrics, distinct cross-lingual performance disparities were identified: LLaMA 3.3 70B recorded the highest ROUGE and BLEU scores in Bengali, while Gemini 2.5 Flash achieved the highest factuality score in Bengali. Model rankings varied significantly depending on the evaluation methodology, demonstrating the limitations of relying on a single evaluation metric and proving that superior performance in English does not directly transfer to low-resource languages. Can LLMs Access their World Knowledge for Event Prediction? This study presents interface design methodologies and a benchmark to help Large Language Models (LLMs) effectively leverage their extensive pre-trained knowledge for time-series event prediction. It identifies that the poor performance of LLMs in event prediction stems not from a lack of knowledge, but from limitations in how input and output interfaces are designed. It proposes two interface design principles: linking each event to a natural language description, and decoupling the semantic meaning of events from the prediction space to prevent bias caused by label association. By establishing and evaluating on DescribeEvent, a new benchmark comprising approximately 150,000 events across seven datasets and six domains, high-performing LLMs achieved superior prediction performance compared to non-LLM baselines and existing methods. Joint Subspace Sparse Attention for Efficient Long-Context Decoding This study proposes JSSA, a token-level joint subspace-based sparse attention method designed to accelerate long-context large language model (LLM) decoding. Implements precise token-level sparse attention by learning a shared low-dimensional query-key subspace that minimizes token score preservation error Develops a gather-free indexed attention kernel supporting independent per-KV-head selection in GQA layouts and eliminating gather operation overhead Significantly enhances decoding throughput while preserving full KV cache-level accuracy across standard GQA, sliding window, and MLA models Pre-Trained Language Models as Zero-Shot Tools for Social Science Research This study demonstrates that pre-trained language models can accurately perform record linkage and name-based demographic inference in a zero-shot manner without task-specific training data. It introduces the EnsembleLink algorithm, which combines pre-trained embeddings with string-based scoring to retrieve candidates and find optimal matches, achieving superior generalization across diverse writing systems and formats. In name-based ethnicity inference using U.S. voter data, LLMs achieved up to 83.8% accuracy, outperforming the conventional name-supplemented BISG method (78.0%). LLMs are also applicable to inferring religion, caste, and other ethnic groups outside the United States, effectively mitigating bias in estimating geographic racial disparities. Apertus 1.5 Release Notes Release announcement of Apertus 1.5, an open-source multimodal model featuring image and audio comprehension, reasoning mode, and a 262k context window expansion. Apertus 1.5 is based on 8B and 70B models and supports early-fusion multimodal understanding via tokenization without requiring a separate vision encoder. Through pre-training and post-training, the context window has expanded to 262,144 tokens (a 4x increase from before), offering an optional Thinking Mode and enhanced tool-use capabilities. It is a fully open-source (Apache 2.0) model with transparently released weights, data source transparency, training details, and core values. Why Does the Pelican Drawn by LLMs Look Like This? Introduces research on the Design Creativity Bench, a UI design creativity benchmark developed to evaluate diversity collapse in LLMs and the limitations of their creative design decisions. The Design Creativity Bench was introduced to evaluate the problem of diversity collapse in LLMs—where similar results are repeatedly generated from the same prompt—within the UI design domain. Across 16 frontier models, three dimensions—originality, creative range, and fitness—were measured and compared against outputs from human designers. Even the top-performing model in the comprehensive creativity evaluation revealed limits in creative capability, showing a substantial gap when compared to human designers. LFM2.5-24B-A2B - High-Speed Inference Model Tailored for Local AI Agents Liquid AI has unveiled LFM2.5-24B-A2B, a high-speed inference MoE model designed for rapid tool calling and multi-step agentic workflows in on-device and local environments. It features an MoE architecture that activates only 2 billion out of 24 billion total parameters per token, supports a 131,072-token context window, and generates an explicit reasoning process prior to the final response. It achieved 184 tokens per second on an Apple M5 Max and 114 tokens per second on an AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395, demonstrating the fastest speeds and lowest memory usage among comparable MoE models in its class. It delivered superior performance compared to rival models on AutomationBench (27.69 points), tool calling, and instruction-following benchmarks, though it slightly lagged behind larger models in knowledge-intensive tasks and coding. Apple: Switch from Chrome to Safari Apple is encouraging users to switch from Chrome to Safari by highlighting Safari's superior loading speed, battery life, privacy protection, and Apple Intelligence convenience features. Apple's internal testing shows that Safari loads websites up to 35% faster and provides up to 5 hours longer video streaming battery life compared to Chrome. It offers robust security and privacy features, including default third-party cookie blocking, Intelligent Tracking Prevention (ITP), and passkey support. It supports Distraction Control to hide unwanted elements, as well as Apple Intelligence-powered features such as webpage change notifications (Notify Me) and tab organization. Show GN: hone - A CLI that automates agent evaluation cycles and benchmarks against unseen cases hone, a CLI tool that automates the cycle of evaluation, test case generation, refinement, and decision-making for AI agent prompt and code modifications, has been released. Based on prompts and code, hone provides capabilities to automatically author test cases (draft), run iterative evaluations alongside reliability analysis (test), and perform root-cause analysis to apply improvements (sharpen). To prevent overfitting, one-third of all test cases are held out for blind evaluation, and results are classified into three tiers—PROVEN, PROMISING, and NO GAIN—while also factoring in cost efficiency. It supports various environments including LangChain, Strands, Python functions, HTTP endpoints, Claude Code/Codex, and MCP servers, and can be installed and used in environments running Bun 1.3.14 or later. Tiny Little Office - A Free Web Office Where the Link Itself Is the Document Introduction to Tiny Little Office, a free web office suite that compresses and stores the entire document content in the URL hash (#), enabling account-free sharing and independent copy editing via just a link Offers 10 tools including word processor, spreadsheet, and presentation, with the entire document content contained in the link itself, allowing sharing without separate accounts or server uploads Does not store documents on a server by default, with each link operating as an independent copy, while supporting offline browser caching and encrypted shortened link storage Supports llms.txt, npx tinylittleoffice CLI, and a skill for Claude Code for AI integration, allowing AI to directly generate document links How artificial neural networks build their own learning machinery [R] A study demonstrating that neural network units following shared plasticity rules can self-organize learning motifs to perform pattern recognition and image classification with performance comparable to backpropagation. Randomly connected neuron-like units can self-organize their own learning circuits via shared plasticity rules. Networks structured in this manner demonstrated performance on par with networks trained using conventional backpropagation algorithms. The researchers emphasized that this mechanism is intended to explore biological plausibility rather than serve as a replacement for backpropagation in large-scale models, noting that it remains unknown whether the actual brain employs this identical mechanism. Google’s new Universal Cart tracks prices on items you want now – plus 3 other tricks For the year-end holiday shopping season, Google unveiled AI-powered 'Universal Cart'—which gathers items from multiple retailers into one place and tracks price fluctuations—along with 'Buying Guide' that helps identify the optimal time to purchase. 'Universal Cart,' allowing users to save and manage items from various retailers in one place via Google Search and the Gemini app, has launched in the US. Based on over 60 billion product listings in the Google Shopping Graph, it automatically provides price drop and restock notifications in the background. It identifies card benefits and promo codes to recommend the best payment method, while supporting direct Google Pay checkout or syncing carts with merchant sites. Can a Radioactive Ore Break China’s Grip on Rare Earths? An article discussing attempts and related challenges in building an alternative supply chain utilizing monazite, a radioactive mineral, to counter China's monopoly on the rare earth market. As China controls approximately 90% of the rare earth oxide and metal markets, regions including North America, Europe, and Australia are actively working to secure alternative supply chains. Monazite ore, rich in neodymium, praseodymium, and dysprosium, is drawing attention as an alternative for rare earths essential across advanced industries such as semiconductors, electric vehicle motors, permanent magnets, and military equipment. Although monazite presents challenges such as strict regulations, specialized processing requirements, and waste management due to its thorium and uranium content, major investments and technological developments are actively underway. Compact Analog Signal Generators for Fast RF Testing This white paper discusses the use of compact analog signal generators that support core RF testing tasks, such as characterization of amplifiers and receivers. It guides methods for measuring amplifier gain, including the P1dB compression point, and analyzing saturation transition characteristics. It introduces techniques for identifying signals caused by nonlinearity through two-tone intermodulation distortion testing and evaluating amplifier linearity using IP3. It presents ways to test receiver performance in blocker environments by simulating real interference signals based on high output power and fast frequency switching speed, and to utilize them as local oscillator (LO) and clock replacements in early development stages. Presentation: What 25 Trillion Tokens Reveal About How Developers Actually Adopt AI Agents A presentation analyzing developer adoption stages and practical approaches to building trust, grounded in large-scale, real-world usage data from the open-source AI coding agent Kilo Code. Rather than blindly chasing model benchmarks, establishing developer trust is what determines the success of real-world AI adoption. Presents strategies for scaling workflows by analyzing key failure points occurring in context construction, multi-model routing, and feedback loops. Examines the discrepancy between industry conventional wisdom and actual developer usage patterns based on real gateway data collected across various environments including CLIs, VS Code extensions, and cloud agents. Drupal Patches Critical Field UI Flaw With Unauthenticated RCE Path via Contributed Modules An urgent patch has been released after a critical security vulnerability was discovered in Drupal core's Field UI module that allows unauthenticated remote code execution (RCE) via contributed modules. Designated as CVE-2026-108097, the vulnerability exists in Drupal core's Field UI module and can lead to unauthenticated remote code execution (RCE) through specific third-party contributed modules. It received a 'Critical' rating with a score of 19 out of 25 in Drupal's own risk assessment, and has been patched in Drupal versions 11.4.10, 11.3.19, and 10.6.19. Sites unable to update immediately can apply a temporary workaround by disabling the Field UI module; however, with 28.5% of sites running unsupported legacy versions (such as 11.2.x or lower, 10.5.x or lower), many remain exposed to security risks. Fake AI Attacks Spread Across US Companies: How MSSPs Can Stop Incidents Early This article discusses how attackers are impersonating well-known AI brands to distribute malware and hijack corporate accounts, as well as early response strategies for Managed Security Service Providers (MSSPs) to counter these threats. Attackers are impersonating major AI services such as Claude, ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot, and Gemini to trick users into running malicious commands or entering credentials on fake pages. By leveraging legitimate Windows utilities (such as mshta.exe) and multi-stage PowerShell execution chains to evade detection, they are increasing the analysis burden and response time for security analysts. MSSPs are facing operational pressure across monitoring, in-depth triage, escalation management, and threat hunting processes, requiring rapid behavioral analysis and intelligence sharing using isolated interactive sandboxes to mitigate these challenges. Why AI Confidence Is Not Validation This article emphasizes that in digital forensics and incident response, high confidence scores from AI outputs should not be blindly trusted and must undergo thorough verification and governance frameworks. Even if an AI tool produces results with a high confidence score, investigators must independently and directly verify the original files and the reasoning process. SANS has published two new AI investigation frameworks aligned with SWGDE and NIST CSF 2.0 standards to ensure AI analysis results can be safely utilized in investigations and legal evidence. AI must not be treated as a single source of truth, and detailed documentation—such as recording the exact names of AI tools used and the precise versions of general-purpose models—is essential. SpaceXSI Unveils Top-Performing 'Grok Voice 3.0'...'Ranked No. 1 in Practical Call Resolution Rate' SpaceXSI has unveiled 'Grok Voice 3.0', a speech-to-speech AI model featuring sub-second response latency and a high practical resolution rate. SpaceXSI unveiled the intelligent voice model 'Grok Voice 3.0', which boasts the highest customer support call resolution rate and sub-second response times. It demonstrated top-tier performance across major benchmarks, including an 86.9 score on Artificial Analysis Speech-to-Speech Index, 97% on Full Duplex Bench, and 74% on ServiceNow EVA-Bench. It is already deployed in real-world services such as Tesla in-vehicle voice assistants and Starlink customer support (approx. 70% automated resolution rate), and is offered via API at the same price as previous versions. Liquid AI Unveils Flagship Model 'LFM2.5-24B-A2B'... \"Targeting On-Device Agents\" Liquid AI has unveiled 'LFM2.5-24B-A2B', a high-speed MoE flagship reasoning model optimized for on-device agent environments. Significantly improved on-device inference speed using an MoE architecture that activates only about 2 billion parameters per token out of a total 24 billion parameters. Significantly outperformed competing models with larger parameter sizes in AutomationBench and real-world agent execution (LiveClaw) evaluations. Released as open weights via Hugging Face and supports various local and server inference frameworks, including llama.cpp and MLX. Eight US States File Lawsuit Against OpenAI, Claiming 'ChatGPT Incites Self-Harm and Violence' Attorneys general from eight US states have filed a major joint lawsuit against OpenAI, alleging that ChatGPT incited self-harm and violence among users and endangered public safety. Attorneys general from eight US states, including Iowa, Kansas, and Arizona, filed lawsuits in their respective state courts seeking civil penalties and damages against OpenAI, alleging that ChatGPT promoted self-harm and violence and caused public harm. The state governments claimed that ChatGPT is designed to excessively validate and engross users, which can reinforce delusions and assist in planning dangerous actions such as shootings, thereby violating consumer protection laws by misleading the public about its safety. The eight states requested that the courts order changes to ChatGPT's operations and demanded that the development of self-improving AI models be halted unless safety aligned with human values is guaranteed. MS Unveils New AI Framework Bridging the 2–6 Week Weather Forecasting Gap Microsoft and researchers have unveiled Probabilistic Bias Correction (PBC), a technology that leverages machine learning to analyze and correct errors in existing weather forecasting models, significantly enhancing the accuracy of 2–6 week subseasonal weather predictions. Developed Probabilistic Bias Correction (PBC) technology that adds a machine learning-based post-processing step atop existing forecasting systems rather than building new large-scale models, published in Nature Demonstrated performance improvements over conventional models in precipitation (98%), temperature (92%), and surface pressure (91%) forecasts when applied to the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF) model, also proving its predictive power for extreme weather events Released as open source and currently being tested to support decision-making for farmers in Kenya as well as deployed in the 'MS Weather' service Mindlogic Launches AI Platform 'BAZE' Targeting Individual Users Beyond Universities and Public Sectors AI startup Mindlogic has launched 'BAZE', a multi-model AI platform targeting individual users, following its B2B business focused on universities and public institutions. Mindlogic officially launched the personal AI platform 'BAZE', expanding its business scope to B2C beyond its existing 'FactChat'-centered B2B and B2G operations. BAZE provides access to approximately 80 generative AI models, including major LLMs such as ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini, as well as video generation models, under a single account for 5,900 KRW per month. Currently supplying services to 100 universities nationwide, the company has reached 750,000 cumulative users, surpassed 10 billion KRW in revenue this year, and recorded a 15-fold year-over-year growth. OpenAI Strikes Back in Apple Trade Secret Lawsuit: \"Our Confidential Data Was Leaked Too\" OpenAI has retaliated against Apple's trade secret infringement lawsuit, claiming that a former employee who returned to Apple leaked confidential files concerning its unreleased AI devices. OpenAI claimed in court that a former employee who returned to Apple leaked over 100 confidential files regarding unreleased AI hardware, hinting at a potential countersuit. Apple had previously filed a lawsuit in July alleging that former employees, including Tang Tan who moved to OpenAI, leaked trade secrets, but Apple flatly denied OpenAI's latest retaliatory claims. The two companies, which formed a partnership in 2024, have entered into a legal dispute as competition intensifies with OpenAI's full-scale development of hardware such as smart speakers and Apple's expansion into home AI devices."